#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f08b8fa-6f05-5c10-b2d7-5d35e6c48e74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product Given the Necessary Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa0ea8b8-d753-5f16-ba5a-1b1d241fd8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa0ea8b8-d753-5f16-ba5a-1b1d241fd8ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524829} :answer-field/id #uuid "0760fbe2-793a-57f7-915e-2969c13795cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08f35ffe-3316-51be-b28c-866b8f0d5426" :question/math-academy-id "q-166144" :question/problem "Compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=2.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))=f(x){g}^{′}(x)+{f}^{′}(x)g(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=2$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=2} & =f(2){g}^{′}(2)+{f}^{′}(2)g(2) \\\\  & =18\\cdot 4+12\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca934a6-9d97-5918-a35b-d823f1e7a22a" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule Given Data For Two Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6a9515f-8460-5af2-b416-a5af4b0fb99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6a9515f-8460-5af2-b416-a5af4b0fb99d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524647} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce65dcc6-4076-500b-9b31-e498469884f3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c5c7d2a8-3068-5036-8a7c-19c736f09c46" :question/math-academy-id "q-166145" :question/problem "Find ${h}^{′}(-2)$, if $h(x)=g(f(x)).$\n\n${h}^{′}(-2)\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(f(x))\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=-2$ and using the values given in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-2) & ={g}^{′}(f(-2))\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(2)\\cdot {f}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c185115e-e3ce-5503-ab5f-a3d0b1a49cfd" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Limit of a Function Given as a Table of Values Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1934568-267c-5dc4-b464-3347e25190ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e1934568-267c-5dc4-b464-3347e25190ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524775} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d5bc92d-ecfb-5f57-9120-28f2a41a99a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ad31a73-80bb-53e8-b8d5-84512ffb448a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166146" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=-2$ directly into the limit, we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2} & ={\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3f(-2)-g(-2)}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2-6}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{{(3f(x)-g(x))}^{′}}{{(x+2)}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}[3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ again, and this time, we are able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}[3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)] & ={[3{f}^{′}(x)-{g}^{′}(x)]|}_{x=-2} \\\\  & =3{f}^{′}(-2)-{g}^{′}(-2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 12-(-4) \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-2}\\frac{3f(x)-g(x)}{x+2}=40.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525043 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064160 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61ffeebf-caf7-5a1f-9fd2-b7d28409246e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ffeebf-caf7-5a1f-9fd2-b7d28409246e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524542} :answer-field/id #uuid "af16ffc8-34d4-5d9f-9c83-e6de0ef51e37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "caea6f22-353c-51f3-b53e-c4874e2c30a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-166147" :question/problem "![](images/7e/7e6d36041e7bd7fe1ecf7cae5f069f5714e06fb546151c7c5d1238219975e811.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=g(x)$ for $x\\in [-4,-1]$ is shown above. Complete the missing part in the statement below.\n\nThe mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $g(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[-4,-1]$ and differentiable on  $(-4,-1).$\n\nGiven that $g(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following: \n\n> *There exists a point $c\\in (a,b)$ such that*\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{g(b)-g(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=-4$ and $b=-1.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{g(-1)-g(-4)}{-1-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-(-1)}{-1+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n>  *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c\\in (-4,-1)$ such that ${g}^{′}(c)=\\frac{2}{3}.$*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "494ef4f7-873c-5d87-85e5-89094c6cc376" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative Given a Table Using a Backward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df2cbeda-e03c-506c-97d7-c7b56df4f483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2cbeda-e03c-506c-97d7-c7b56df4f483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524763} :answer-field/id #uuid "281ee34a-5ec1-50ca-8410-459bfa2da35a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93c6d227-1e4b-58a1-bf67-7c22cb760172" :question/math-academy-id "q-166148" :question/problem "Given that $f(2.1)=21,$ approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=2.1$ using a backward difference approximation.\n\n${f}^{′}(2.1)\\,≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We use the backward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x)-f(x-h)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=2.1.$ \n\nWe choose the step size $h>0$ to be as small as possible. In this case, $h=0.1$ since the nearest backward point, for which we know the value of $f(x),$ is $f(2)=18$ (from the table above).\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2.1) & ≈\\frac{f(2.1)-f(2.1-0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =10(f(2.1)-f(2)) \\\\  & =10(21-18) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119954 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "744b0e69-71f9-5ca0-a200-8968a9ec192e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5eab96d-676d-5a2c-9e29-604cf8b1f1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5eab96d-676d-5a2c-9e29-604cf8b1f1f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524816} :answer-field/id #uuid "17463037-04b2-54df-9864-31c60a5f05a8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd542e6d-453b-52cc-8c0f-6c5dc531d308" :question/math-academy-id "q-166149" :question/problem "Find the vertical asymptotes of $y=r(x),$ where $r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.$\n\nThe vertical asymptote is $x=$\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that \n\n$$\ny=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, we find the vertical asymptotes by setting the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n5x-2=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ is the only vertical asymptote of $y=r(x),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/61/61d7b22d6df4c7cb85a814e84a8af2f8d162fe75d25b4e3e09258bfe965719ff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119939 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ac6edc-908a-50be-9ef1-4b24ba597d1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating dy/dx Using dx/dy"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a5abe1-8a17-5f77-aca0-0fecc6ce44b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(5y-2)^2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03a5abe1-8a17-5f77-aca0-0fecc6ce44b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(5y-2)^2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524382} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ee6ce2-448a-5e5a-a2ca-c421a7bb8463" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "35035d93-2432-5072-a261-b3e11d036f56" :question/math-academy-id "q-166151" :question/problem "Given that $x=\\frac{3}{5y-2},$ write $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ in terms of $y.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we differentiate $x=\\frac{3}{5y-2}$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{3}{5y-2}) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}((5y-2)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-3(5y-2)^{-2}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{(5y-2)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{15}(5y-2)^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0196af83-9ef6-5bab-9f50-0aacf488f0cc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of the Inverse of a Composite Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4197bd9-e830-5e33-b585-2e4d2a3b0a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4197bd9-e830-5e33-b585-2e4d2a3b0a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524781} :answer-field/id #uuid "240c4af1-601a-56e4-8a24-6ce123e0c99f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "931a5d58-e545-53ef-9d43-7a3c38508bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "931a5d58-e545-53ef-9d43-7a3c38508bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524615} :answer-field/id #uuid "7183c527-e212-5bc2-a3ad-d8fa7c83c6fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da95b5d5-ad91-5dbb-9815-db7c7936c826" :question/math-academy-id "q-166152" :question/problem "Write ${({r}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${r}^{-1}(x).$\n\n$({r}^{-1})^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}} \n$\\cdot (5{r}^{-1}(x)-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=r(x)=\\frac{3}{5x-2},$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{5y-2}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={r}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we need to differentiate with respect to $y$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ In part 7, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{15}(5y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${r}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={r}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{r}^{-1}(x)] & =-\\frac{1}{15}{(5{r}^{-1}(x)-2)}^{2} \\\\ ({r}^{-1})^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\cdot {(5{r}^{-1}(x)-\\,2)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
